Task 1: Build the Inventory and Roles
Subtask 1.1: Create the Project Structure and Inventory
Confirm you are on the control node:
whoami
# Should show: ansible-user
Create the project directory structure:
mkdir -p ~/comprehensive-project/{inventories,roles,playbooks,group_vars,scripts,templates}
cd ~/comprehensive-project
Create the inventory file:
vim inventories/production.yml
---
all:
  children:
    webservers:
      hosts:
        web-server-1:
          ansible_host: 192.168.1.10
          server_role: primary
        web-server-2:
          ansible_host: 192.168.1.11
          server_role: secondary
      vars:
        http_port: 80
        document_root: /var/www/html

    databases:
      hosts:
        db-server-1:
          ansible_host: 192.168.1.20
          db_role: master
      vars:
        db_port: 3306

  vars:
    ansible_user: ansible-user
    ansible_ssh_private_key_file: ~/.ssh/id_rsa
    environment: production
Create group variables for webservers:
vim group_vars/webservers.yml
---
packages:
  - apache2
  - php
  - libapache2-mod-php
  - php-mysql

firewall_rules:
  - port: "{{ http_port }}"
    protocol: tcp
Create group variables for databases:
vim group_vars/databases.yml
---
packages:
  - mysql-server
  - python3-pymysql

mysql_root_password: "SecurePassword123!"

mysql_databases:
  - name: webapp_db
    encoding: utf8mb4
    collation: utf8mb4_unicode_ci

mysql_users:
  - name: webapp_user
    password: "WebAppPassword456!"
    priv: "webapp_db.*:ALL"
    host: "%"
Test connectivity and inventory structure:
ansible all -i inventories/production.yml -m ping
ansible-inventory -i inventories/production.yml --list
Deliverable: A working inventories/production.yml with two groups (webservers, databases), plus group_vars/webservers.yml and group_vars/databases.yml. ansible all -m ping returns "ping": "pong" for all three managed nodes, and ansible-inventory --list shows the correct group membership and host variables.

Subtask 1.2: Create the Webserver Role
Generate the role skeleton:
ansible-galaxy init roles/webserver
Define webserver tasks:
vim roles/webserver/tasks/main.yml
---
- name: Install web server packages
  apt:
    name: "{{ packages }}"
    state: present
    update_cache: yes
  become: yes

- name: Start and enable Apache service
  service:
    name: apache2
    state: started
    enabled: yes
  become: yes

- name: Deploy web application page
  template:
    src: index.php.j2
    dest: "{{ document_root }}/index.php"
    owner: www-data
    group: www-data
    mode: '0644'
  become: yes
  notify: restart apache
Create the application template:
vim roles/webserver/templates/index.php.j2
<?php
echo "<h1>Welcome to " . gethostname() . "</h1>";
echo "<p>Server Role: {{ server_role }}</p>";
echo "<p>Environment: {{ environment }}</p>";
echo "<p>Server IP: {{ ansible_default_ipv4.address }}</p>";
echo "<p>Current Time: " . date('Y-m-d H:i:s') . "</p>";
?>
Create the handler:
vim roles/webserver/handlers/main.yml
---
- name: restart apache
  service:
    name: apache2
    state: restarted
  become: yes
Deliverable: A complete roles/webserver directory containing tasks/main.yml, templates/index.php.j2, and handlers/main.yml. Running ansible-playbook --syntax-check against a playbook that includes this role produces no errors (verified in Task 2).

Subtask 1.3: Create the Database Role
Generate the role skeleton:
ansible-galaxy init roles/database
Define database tasks:
vim roles/database/tasks/main.yml
---
- name: Install MySQL packages
  apt:
    name: "{{ packages }}"
    state: present
    update_cache: yes
  become: yes

- name: Start and enable MySQL service
  service:
    name: mysql
    state: started
    enabled: yes
  become: yes

- name: Set MySQL root password
  mysql_user:
    name: root
    password: "{{ mysql_root_password }}"
    login_unix_socket: /var/run/mysqld/mysqld.sock
    check_implicit_admin: yes
    state: present
  become: yes

- name: Create application databases
  mysql_db:
    name: "{{ item.name }}"
    encoding: "{{ item.encoding | default('utf8mb4') }}"
    collation: "{{ item.collation | default('utf8mb4_unicode_ci') }}"
    login_user: root
    login_password: "{{ mysql_root_password }}"
    state: present
  loop: "{{ mysql_databases }}"
  become: yes

- name: Create application database users
  mysql_user:
    name: "{{ item.name }}"
    password: "{{ item.password }}"
    priv: "{{ item.priv }}"
    host: "{{ item.host | default('localhost') }}"
    login_user: root
    login_password: "{{ mysql_root_password }}"
    state: present
  loop: "{{ mysql_users }}"
  become: yes
Create the handler:
vim roles/database/handlers/main.yml
---
- name: restart mysql
  service:
    name: mysql
    state: restarted
  become: yes
Deliverable: A complete roles/database directory containing tasks/main.yml and handlers/main.yml, using variables defined in group_vars/databases.yml.

Task 2: Build and Run the Master Playbook
Subtask 2.1: Create the Deployment Playbook
Create the master playbook:
vim playbooks/deploy-infrastructure.yml
---
- name: Deploy Web Servers
  hosts: webservers
  become: yes

  pre_tasks:
    - name: Display deployment information
      debug:
        msg: "Deploying to {{ inventory_hostname }} in group(s) {{ group_names }}"

  roles:
    - webserver

  post_tasks:
    - name: Verify Apache is running
      service:
        name: apache2
        state: started
      check_mode: yes
      register: apache_status

    - name: Display Apache status
      debug:
        msg: "Apache change required: {{ apache_status.changed }}"

- name: Deploy Database Server
  hosts: databases
  become: yes
  serial: 1

  roles:
    - database

  post_tasks:
    - name: Verify MySQL is running
      service:
        name: mysql
        state: started
      check_mode: yes
      register: mysql_status

    - name: Display MySQL status
      debug:
        msg: "MySQL change required: {{ mysql_status.changed }}"
Run a syntax check before executing anything:
ansible-playbook -i inventories/production.yml playbooks/deploy-infrastructure.yml --syntax-check
Expected output: playbook: playbooks/deploy-infrastructure.yml with no errors.

Run the playbook in check mode (dry run) first:
ansible-playbook -i inventories/production.yml playbooks/deploy-infrastructure.yml --check --diff
Execute the real deployment:
ansible-playbook -i inventories/production.yml playbooks/deploy-infrastructure.yml -v
Deliverable: A successful ansible-playbook run showing PLAY RECAP with failed=0 for all three hosts. Visiting http://192.168.1.10 (or using curl from the control node) returns the rendered PHP page showing the hostname, server role, and environment.

Subtask 2.2: Verify the Deployment
Check the deployed page from the control node:
ansible webservers -i inventories/production.yml -m uri -a "url=http://{{ ansible_host }}/index.php return_content=yes"
Confirm the database and application user exist:
ansible databases -i inventories/production.yml -m shell \
  -a "mysql -u root -p'SecurePassword123!' -e 'SHOW DATABASES;'" --become
Deliverable: The uri module output shows status: 200 for both web servers, and the SHOW DATABASES output includes webapp_db.

Task 3: Debug and Troubleshoot the Deployment
Subtask 3.1: Use Verbosity and Ad Hoc Commands to Diagnose Issues
Re-run the playbook with increased verbosity to inspect module arguments and results:
ansible-playbook -i inventories/production.yml playbooks/deploy-infrastructure.yml -vvv
Look for the TASK [webserver : Deploy web application page] section and confirm the rendered template values match your inventory variables.

Use --start-at-task to re-run from a specific point without repeating earlier steps:
ansible-playbook -i inventories/production.yml playbooks/deploy-infrastructure.yml \
  --start-at-task="Deploy web application page"
Use an ad hoc command to confirm the Apache service state directly on the host:
ansible webservers -i inventories/production.yml -a "systemctl is-active apache2" --become
Expected output: active for each web server.

Deliverable: A terminal transcript showing -vvv output for the template task, a successful --start-at-task re-run, and active returned from the ad hoc systemctl check.

Subtask 3.2: Build a Debugging Playbook
Create a debugging playbook that inspects logs and confirms database sizes:
vim playbooks/debug-infrastructure.yml
---
- name: Debug Web Services
  hosts: webservers
  become: yes

  tasks:
    - name: Check Apache configuration syntax
      command: apache2ctl configtest
      register: apache_config_test
      changed_when: false
      failed_when: false

    - name: Display Apache configuration test result
      debug:
        msg: "{{ apache_config_test.stderr_lines }}"

    - name: Check recent Apache error log entries
      command: tail -n 20 /var/log/apache2/error.log
      register: apache_errors
      changed_when: false
      failed_when: false

    - name: Display recent Apache errors
      debug:
        msg: "{{ apache_errors.stdout_lines }}"

- name: Debug Database Service
  hosts: databases
  become: yes

  tasks:
    - name: Check MySQL service status
      command: systemctl status mysql
      register: mysql_status
      changed_when: false
      failed_when: false

    - name: Display MySQL service status
      debug:
        msg: "{{ mysql_status.stdout_lines }}"

    - name: Check database sizes
      command: >
        mysql -u root -p{{ mysql_root_password }} -e
        "SELECT table_schema AS Database, SUM(data_length + index_length) / 1024 / 1024 AS Size_MB FROM information_schema.tables GROUP BY table_schema;"
      register: db_sizes
      changed_when: false
      no_log: true

    - name: Display database sizes
      debug:
        msg: "{{ db_sizes.stdout_lines }}"
Run the debugging playbook:
ansible-playbook -i inventories/production.yml playbooks/debug-infrastructure.yml
Deliverable: The playbook completes with failed=0, the "Display database sizes" task shows a table listing each schema (including webapp_db and information_schema) with its size in megabytes, and the Apache debug tasks show Syntax OK in the configtest output.

Expected Outcomes
A complete, syntactically valid Ansible project with a structured inventory, webserver and database roles, and a working master playbook
A successful, idempotent deployment of Apache and MySQL across three managed nodes, verified by HTTP checks and SQL queries
Practical experience using --syntax-check, --check --diff, -vvv, and --start-at-task to debug playbook execution before and after a run
Troubleshooting
Symptom: ansible-playbook fails with UNREACHABLE for one or more hosts. Diagnostic hint: Run ansible all -i inventories/production.yml -m ping -vvv and check whether the SSH key path in the inventory vars section matches the actual key on the control node.
Symptom: The mysql_user or mysql_db tasks fail with an authentication error on a second playbook run. Diagnostic hint: The root password may already be set from a previous run while the task still uses the unix socket login; check whether login_unix_socket is still valid after the first password change and adjust the login method accordingly.
Conclusion
In this lab you built a complete, self-contained Ansible project from the ground up: a structured inventory with host and group variables, two purpose-built roles for web and database servers, and a master playbook that deploys and verifies both tiers in sequence. You then practiced the core debugging workflow every Ansible practitioner relies on in production â�� syntax checking, dry runs with --check --diff, verbose output with -vvv, targeted re-runs with --start-at-task, and a dedicated debugging playbook that inspects service status, logs, and database size directly. These skills translate directly to managing larger, real-world infrastructure, where the same patterns of modular roles, layered variables, and disciplined troubleshooting scale to dozens or hundreds of hosts. As a next step, consider extending this project with Ansible Vault to encrypt the MySQL passwords stored in group_vars, or explore Ansible Tower/AWX for scheduling and auditing these same playbooks in a team environment.
